  "evidence": "TEXT_F: NHS over £30bn extra; end privatisation; safe staffing; meet A&E targets; free hospital parking. Social care: £8bn and foundations of a National Care Service with capped costs. Work/cost of living: £10 Living Wage by 2020; ban zero‑hours; rights from day one; end public‑sector pay cap; cap household energy bills; bring utilities/rail into public ownership with fare caps. Housing: at least 100k social homes per year; three‑year tenancies; cap rent rises; end rough sleeping. Education: abolish tuition fees; restore maintenance grants; expand childcare/Sure Start. Brexit: jobs‑first approach retaining Single Market/Customs Union benefits and guaranteeing EU nationals’ rights. Policing: 10,000 more police. Tax: no rises for 95% of earners.\nTEXT_D: Second referendum on the final Brexit deal; stay in single market/customs union. NHS/social care: 1p on Income Tax to raise £6bn; integrate health and care; workforce/mental health measures. Education: nearly £7bn extra; oppose grammars; reinstate maintenance grants; free primary meals; breakfast clubs. Housing: 300k homes/year; renters’ reforms; end rough sleeping via Housing First. Workers’ rights: tackle zero‑hours abuse; scrap tribunal fees; gig‑economy reforms. Environment: diesel ban by 2025; net‑zero by 2050.",
  "reasoning": "Voters in 2017 prioritised the NHS, living standards and wages, affordable housing, and a pragmatic Brexit; TEXT_F offers larger, more concrete commitments on NHS and social care funding, wages/tenancy protections, cost‑of‑living (energy caps, public ownership), and a jobs‑first Brexit. TEXT_D aligns on services and housing but centers a second referendum and smaller immediate funding, making it less squarely targeted at those dominant voter priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
